**1. Summary of the PDF Research Matter (Journal of Controlled Release 204 (2015) 51-59 and npj Vaccines (2017)2:4):**
The research focuses on Nano-11, a positively charged, dendrimer-like α-D-glucan nanoparticle derived from phytoglycogen, as a novel vaccine adjuvant. These studies demonstrate Nano-11's ability to effectively deliver antigens to dendritic cells (DCs) by adsorption, leading to DC activation marked by increased expression of costimulatory molecules (CD80, CD86) and secretion of pro-inflammatory cytokines like IL-1β. In vivo, Nano-11 significantly enhances antigen-specific antibody responses, comparable or superior to aluminum hydroxide adjuvant, particularly at lower doses. Nano-11 remains localized at the injection site, inducing a transient inflammatory response characterized by monocytes/macrophages, and efficiently targets antigens to migratory DCs in draining lymph nodes. The adjuvant exhibits a favorable safety profile with gradual clearance and no systemic distribution.

